WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.793
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.093 --> 00:00:16.552
Unit seven: Complex Problem Solving. Today you will reason about consequences with abstract inverted conditionals.

4
00:00:16.852 --> 00:00:19.481
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:23.620
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:25.659
Systems thinking.

7
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.751
Systems thinking means analysing how parts of a system interact as a whole.

8
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:34.580
Systems thinking showed that more roads increased congestion.

9
00:00:35.183 --> 00:00:38.033
Your turn. Say: Systems thinking.

10
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.297
Systems thinking.

11
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.295
Root cause.

12
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:48.990
Root cause means the fundamental reason a problem occurs.

13
00:00:49.583 --> 00:00:53.255
Poor drainage, not rainfall, was the root cause of the flooding.

14
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.804
Your turn. Say: Root cause.

15
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:00.842
Root cause.

16
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:02.910
Interdisciplinary.

17
00:01:03.917 --> 00:01:07.460
Interdisciplinary means combining several academic fields.

18
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:12.360
An interdisciplinary team of engineers and sociologists studied the slum.

19
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.055
Your turn. Say: Interdisciplinary.

20
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.261
Interdisciplinary.

21
00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:22.517
Trade-off.

22
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:27.780
Trade-off means a balance between two desirable but competing things.

23
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:32.368
Every policy involves a trade-off between cost and coverage.

24
00:01:32.983 --> 00:01:36.460
Your turn. Say: Trade-off.

25
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.056
Trade-off.

26
00:01:41.817 --> 00:01:43.340
Iterative process.

27
00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:47.766
Iterative process means a cycle of repeated testing and refinement.

28
00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.383
Design is an iterative process of trial and correction.

29
00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:54.996
Your turn. Say: Iterative process.

30
00:01:57.833 --> 00:01:59.357
Iterative process.

31
00:02:00.117 --> 00:02:01.977
Unintended consequence.

32
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:06.875
Unintended consequence means an unforeseen result of an action.

33
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:11.480
An unintended consequence of the plastic ban was more paper waste.

34
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:15.479
Your turn. Say: Unintended consequence.

35
00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:19.996
Unintended consequence.

36
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.157
Holistic approach.

37
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.145
Holistic approach means considering the whole situation rather than isolated parts.

38
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.654
A holistic approach addressed health, income and education together.

39
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.576
Your turn. Say: Holistic approach.

40
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:40.794
Holistic approach.

41
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.132
Adaptive strategy.

42
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:48.125
Adaptive strategy means a plan designed to change as conditions change.

43
00:02:48.717 --> 00:02:53.073
Farmers adopted an adaptive strategy as rainfall became unpredictable.

44
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:57.590
Your turn. Say: Adaptive strategy.

45
00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:02.027
Adaptive strategy.

46
00:03:02.783 --> 00:03:03.761
Heuristic.

47
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:07.632
Heuristic means a mental shortcut or rule of thumb.

48
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:11.273
'Follow the money' is a useful heuristic for investigators.

49
00:03:11.883 --> 00:03:14.524
Your turn. Say: Heuristic.

50
00:03:17.367 --> 00:03:18.368
Heuristic.

51
00:03:19.117 --> 00:03:20.315
Contingency.

52
00:03:21.317 --> 00:03:25.278
Contingency means a possible future event that must be planned for.

53
00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:29.114
The plan covered every contingency except a pandemic.

54
00:03:29.717 --> 00:03:32.450
Your turn. Say: Contingency.

55
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.475
Contingency.

56
00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:41.010
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:47.057
Visal, Phnom Penh wants to reduce traffic jams. Why not simply build wider roads?

58
00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:56.770
Because systems thinking predicts induced demand. Were roads widened, driving would become easier, attracting even more cars.

59
00:03:57.883 --> 00:03:59.581
So what's the root cause?

60
00:04:00.683 --> 00:04:11.846
It's interconnected: limited public transport, cheap fuel and car-centred planning. Were we to tackle only one factor, the others would simply compensate.

61
00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:14.915
Then the problem seems impossible.

62
00:04:16.017 --> 00:04:25.380
Not impossible, but it demands an iterative, holistic approach. Should a pilot bus lane succeed, it could be expanded step by step.

63
00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:27.929
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:28.783 --> 00:04:31.712
Grammar focus: Abstract conditional reasoning.

65
00:04:32.012 --> 00:04:33.129
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:33.817 --> 00:04:38.266
Were we to ignore trade-offs, unintended consequences would follow.

67
00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:42.148
Were roads widened, more cars would be attracted.

68
00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:46.629
Should a pilot succeed, it could be expanded gradually.

69
00:04:47.567 --> 00:04:58.401
'Were + subject + to-infinitive' presents a hypothetical action (Were we to ignore…); 'Should + subject + verb' presents a less likely future possibility (Should it fail…). Both are formal alternatives to 'if'.

70
00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.254
Be careful! Don't say: Should the plan fails, we adapt.

71
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.291
Say: Should the plan fail, we will adapt.

72
00:05:08.591 --> 00:05:10.741
Should + subject + base verb.

73
00:05:11.533 --> 00:05:14.279
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:16.335
Wicked Problems.

75
00:05:16.635 --> 00:05:25.531
Complex problems such as urban congestion, climate adaptation and poverty are sometimes described as 'wicked', because they lack a single definition or solution.

76
00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:34.570
Each intervention changes the system, so were policymakers to treat symptoms in isolation, unintended consequences would almost certainly follow.

77
00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:42.686
Systems thinking encourages analysts to map feedback loops and identify root causes rather than the most visible effects.

78
00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:51.876
Because uncertainty is unavoidable, effective solutions tend to be iterative, combining small experiments with continuous evaluation.

79
00:05:52.867 --> 00:06:01.577
Interdisciplinary teams are particularly valuable, since engineers, economists and community members each perceive different parts of the same system.

80
00:06:02.667 --> 00:06:06.564
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:09.997
blank we to cut taxes, revenue would fall.

82
00:06:10.297 --> 00:06:13.288
A, Were. B, Was. C, Should.

83
00:06:16.833 --> 00:06:18.543
The answer is A: Were.

84
00:06:19.683 --> 00:06:22.623
The fundamental reason a problem occurs is the blank.

85
00:06:22.923 --> 00:06:26.913
A, heuristic. B, root cause. C, contingency.

86
00:06:30.467 --> 00:06:33.012
The answer is B: root cause.

87
00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:36.765
An unforeseen result of an action is an blank.

88
00:06:37.065 --> 00:06:43.656
A, adaptive strategy. B, iterative process. C, unintended consequence.

89
00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.384
The answer is C: unintended consequence.

90
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.375
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:53.675 --> 00:07:01.288
A, Should the trial fails, we will stop.. B, Should the trial fail, we will stop.. C, If should the trial fail, we stop..

92
00:07:04.833 --> 00:07:08.214
The answer is B: Should the trial fail, we will stop..

93
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.685
According to the reading, wicked problems are difficult because they…

94
00:07:12.985 --> 00:07:19.401
A, lack a single definition or solution. B, are very small. C, affect only engineers.

95
00:07:22.967 --> 00:07:26.917
The answer is A: lack a single definition or solution.

96
00:07:28.133 --> 00:07:31.355
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:34.971
How would you approach a complex, multi-layered problem?

98
00:07:51.783 --> 00:07:53.237
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:53.537 --> 00:08:28.520
I would begin by resisting the temptation to act immediately on the most visible symptom. First, I would map the system, identifying stakeholders, feedback loops and possible root causes, ideally with an interdisciplinary team. Next, I would test small interventions, because were I to launch a large solution without evidence, unintended consequences could outweigh the benefits. Finally, I would monitor results and adapt continuously. Complex problems are rarely solved once; they are managed iteratively.

100
00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:30.494
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:31.383 --> 00:08:35.998
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and abstract conditional reasoning.

102
00:08:36.298 --> 00:08:41.086
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

103
00:08:41.386 --> 00:08:43.656
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

104
00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:47.119
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

